-- Generated by drizzle-kit (the table, the foreign keys, the indexes and the check constraints),
-- then edited by hand: the guards on capability_ref, on duplicate policy decisions and on revoked
-- connections' leftover secrets, the type change with an explicit cast, the data migration of
-- revoked connections' secrets, and the two triggers, which drizzle-kit cannot
-- express (the same split as 0002 and 0004). ADR-014 section 11, docs/phase-3.md step B1.
--
-- 1. execution_jobs.capability_ref was an unused text column in Phase 2. It becomes a uuid with a
--    foreign key to vault_access_capabilities. The migration refuses to run if any row holds a
--    value, instead of casting whatever is there: nothing in Phase 2 wrote the column, so a value
--    means someone edited the database by hand and a person should look at it first.
-- 2. vault_secrets.status gains the terminal status `revoked`. Secrets of connections that are
--    already revoked move to it (Phase 2 revocation left them `deleted`). Two triggers keep it
--    terminal: no UPDATE may move a row out of `revoked` or give a revoked secret an active
--    version, and no version row may be inserted for a revoked secret.
--    LIMITATION (as for audit_events in 0002): the table owner or a superuser can disable the
--    triggers. The vault's own statements also require status <> 'revoked', so the trigger is a
--    backstop against a writer that bypasses the repositories, not the only check.
--    The migration refuses to run when a revoked connection's secret still has an active version
--    pointer or version rows: Phase 2 revocation deleted them, so ciphertext left behind means
--    the database was edited by hand or revocation failed half way, and a person should look at
--    it before the rows are marked terminal.
-- 3. execution_jobs_error_code_check gains vault_unavailable and vault_denied.
-- 4. execution_jobs.policy_decision_id becomes unique (partial, NULLs allowed): one policy
--    decision authorizes at most one job. The migration refuses to run if Phase 2 data already
--    has two jobs on one decision (the execute route writes one decision event per request, so
--    this means a hand edit).
DO $$
BEGIN
  IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM "execution_jobs" WHERE "capability_ref" IS NOT NULL) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION '0005_phase3_vault: execution_jobs.capability_ref holds values, but Phase 2 never wrote it; refusing to convert it to uuid'
      USING ERRCODE = 'data_exception';
  END IF;
END;
$$;
